window.docContents[115].resolve({"/releases/#blog-example-subprojects":{"contents":"When using the anchore feature, code from a separate Lean project should be written in ordinary Lean modules.\nThis subproject should still require SubVerso, which Verso uses to extract the highlighted code.\nUse comments of the form -- ANCHOR: NAME and -- ANCHOR_END: NAME to mark named regions of a module.\nThere's no requirement that anchors be well-nested or that the follow the syntax tree.\n\nTo migrate a post:* In the example code, replace each %example name and %end pair with -- ANCHOR: name and -- ANCHOR_END: name, and remove the SubVerso.Examples import.* In the post, open Verso.Code.External and set the options verso.exampleProject to the project's directory and verso.exampleModule to the module's name.\n  The named argument module selects a different module of the same project.* Use the anchor code block in place of leanCommand.\n  The code block should contain the code that it's referring to.\n  If there's a mismatch, Verso will offer to update it.* Use the anchorTerm and anchorName roles in place of leanTerm.\n  They refer to a named anchor in the file, while the code with the role selects a particular term or name found in the file.* Use the anchorInfo, anchorWarning, and anchorError code blocks to show an anchor's messages.\n\nA name shown with %show_name should be migrated to an anchor around an example or #check that mentions the name.\nIn the post, it can be quoted with anchorName.\nA sub-term example such as %ex{name}{term} should be migrated to an anchor around the code, referred to via anchorTerm.\nTo migrate %signature, wrap an anchor around a command that restates the declaration's signature, such as Mathlib's recall.\n\n\n","context":"Writing Documentation in Lean with Verso\u0009Release Notes\u0009Verso 4.35.0","header":"Blog Example Subprojects Removed","id":"/releases/#blog-example-subprojects"}});